[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Module Design Patterns & Composition in Terraform"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"As infrastructure as code bases grow, managing complexity becomes critical. Terraform modules are the primary mechanism for code reuse, encapsulation, and organization. This tutorial explores advanced design patterns and composition strategies for building scalable, maintainable Terraform modules."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. The Wrapper Module Pattern"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Wrapper Module pattern involves creating a localized module that calls a foundational or community module, pre-configuring it with organizational defaults, compliance settings, and standard tagging. This reduces boilerplate code across multiple teams while enforcing governance."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"module "secure_s3_bucket" {
  source = "app.terraform.io/example-org/s3-bucket/aws"
  version = "2.1.0"

  bucket_name = var.bucket_name
  force_destroy = false
  enable_versioning = true
  server_side_encryption = "AES256"

  tags = merge(var.standard_tags, {
    ManagedBy = "Terraform"
  })
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Module Composition and Chaining"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Composition allows you to combine smaller, single-purpose modules to build complex architectures. Instead of creating massive monolithic modules, you pass the output of one module as an input to another."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"module "vpc" {
  source = "./modules/vpc"
  cidr_block = "10.0.0.0/16"
}

module "compute" {
  source = "./modules/compute"
  subnet_id = module.vpc.public_subnet_ids[0]
  instance_type = "t3.micro"
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Best Practices for Interface Design"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep input variables (arguments) focused and validate them using validation blocks to fail fast."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Expose meaningful outputs. Only output resource attributes that downstream modules or root configurations actually need."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid deep module nesting (more than 2 or 3 levels), as it severely complicates debugging and state management."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"By applying wrapper patterns, keeping modules decoupled, and composing them deliberately, your infrastructure code remains modular, testable, and aligned with enterprise standards."}]
